  "text": "**Screening the four solutions**\n\n- **Solution 2 is rejected.** It answers a weak-Sidon-set question (min Sidon subset size, ratio 2/3), which is a different problem from the ℓ^p dynamics.\n- **Solution 3 is rejected.** It gives β_p = 3 for all p, which would make the \"p < 3\" qualifier pointless. Its p=2 claim also fails. For p=2 this is random Gauss-Seidel on the Laplacian, and on a barbell-type graph the relevant killed-walk hitting time is about n³, so each walk step costs about n updates and the time is about n⁴. Its path-only argument never considers this worst case.\n- **Solution 4 is rejected.** Its formula max(3, p/(p−1)) gives 3 at p=2, which has the same flaw as Solution 3. Its transition at p = 3/2 also conflicts with the problem's own threshold at p = 3.\n- **Solution 1 survives.** Its barbell/bottleneck heuristic is hand-waved, not proven. Still, its p=2 value of 4 matches the hitting-time heuristic above, and its transition to the path exponent 3 happens exactly at p = 3, matching the problem's threshold. Since 2p/(p−1) is the only candidate here that fits both checks, I select it, with moderate confidence.\n\n**Answer:** β_p = 2p/(p−1) for 1 < p < 3, from Solution 1.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
